  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Let SSS be a string of length NNN, containing only the characters `'L'` and `'R'`, with the following properties:\n\n\nN≥3N \\ge 3N≥3\nS1=S2=RS_1 = S_2 = \\text{R}S1​=S2​=R\nSN=LS_N = \\text{L}SN​=L\nThere is also a strip of NNN cells, numbered 111 to NNN from left to right.\n\nSuppose you are standing at position XXX on the strip. Then,\n\n\nIf SX=RS_X = \\text{R}SX​=R, you will move one step right.\nIf SX=LS_X = \\text{L}SX​=L, you will move two steps left.\nIt can be proved that with the initial conditions imposed on the string SSS, your position will always lie in [1,N][1, N][1,N].\n\nYou don't know the string SSS.\n\nInstead, you are given NNN and an array AAA of length NNN, which represents the following:\n\n\nFor each iii (1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N), if you start at position iii and follow the movement rules **exactly** NNN times, you will end up at position AiA_iAi​.\nDetermine if there exists a string SSS of length NNN such that S1=S2=RS_1 = S_2 = \\text{R}S1​=S2​=R and SN=LS_N = \\text{L}SN​=L such that moving according to it will result in the given array AAA.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the length of the array.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: `Yes` if a valid string SSS exists, and `No` otherwise.\n\n\nEach letter of the output may be printed in either uppercase or lowercase, i.e. the strings `NO`, `No`, `nO`, and `no` will be considered equivalent.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "3≤N≤3⋅1053 \\leq N \\leq 3\\cdot 10^53≤N≤3⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤N1 \\le A_i \\le N1≤Ai​≤N",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 3⋅1053\\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3\n1 2 3\n3\n3 2 1\n5\n3 4 2 3 4\n6\n4 5 6 1 2 3",
        "output": "Yes\nNo\nYes\nNo",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The string S=RRLS = \\text{RRL}S=RRL will result in A=[1,2,3]A = [1, 2, 3]A=[1,2,3], because:\n\nStarting at i=1i = 1i=1 and following the movement N=3N =3N=3 times, we'll move right once, right once again, and then left two steps, ending back at 111.\nStarting at i=2i = 2i=2 and following the movement N=3N=3N=3 times, we'll move right once, left two steps, and right once again, ending at 222.\nStarting at i=3i = 3i=3 and following the movement N=3N=3N=3 times, we'll move left two steps, right once, and right once again, ending at 333.\nSo, A=[1,2,3]A = [1, 2, 3]A=[1,2,3], as desired.\n\nTest case 222: It can be proved that no string of length 333 results in A=[3,2,1]A = [3, 2, 1]A=[3,2,1]."
      }
    ]
